{
  "dom_id": "step-q162800",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$ given that ${x}^{2}+y\\le 2.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+y=2.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=2\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives us the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -4y=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ y=-\\frac{\\lambda }{4}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=2$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation gives $y=-\\frac{1}{4},$ and the constraint condition $g(x,y)=2$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4} & =2\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-8$ | $\\frac{17}{8}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{17}{8}.$",
